Chapter: ProbeMatchGuard

 A guarded match arm, and nothing else. The zig plug never reads
 IRBranch.guard -- the word does not appear in ZigEmitter.codex -- so a guarded
 arm is emitted as a switch prong with no condition and fires whenever its
 PATTERN matches. Bare metal honours the guard (X86_64Compound.codex,
 emit-match-guard).

 The shape is chosen so the difference is a wrong ANSWER rather than a compile
 error. Two arms on the same constructor would be duplicate switch prongs,
 which zig rejects, and a refusal is the lucky case. One guarded arm plus a
 catch-all compiles cleanly on both arms and disagrees.

 Expected on bare metal:

   guard-taken 0
   guard-taken 1
   otherwise 0

 A plug that drops the guard prints 1 on the first line: the .Num prong
 matches Num 5 and fires unconditionally.

 The first line is the whole test: classify 5 must NOT take the negative arm.
 If the plug drops the guard, it prints 1 there and the arms disagree at that
 line.

 No cites, single chapter, ASCII only, so it can ride as a ladder subject or
 compile standalone with build/compile.ps1.

 We say:

Section: Types

  Val =
    | Num (Integer)
    | Word (Text)

Section: Classify

 classify answers 1 when it took the guarded arm, 0 when it fell through to the
 unguarded one for the same constructor, and 2 for a different constructor.
 Numbers rather than text so the answer cannot be confused with a print path.

  classify : Val -> Integer
  classify (v) =
   when v
    is Num (n) when n < 0 -> 1
    is otherwise -> 0

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("guard-taken " & show (classify (Num 5)))
    print-line-uni ("guard-taken " & show (classify (Num -5)))
    print-line-uni ("otherwise " & show (classify (Word "x")))
  end
